\f1\fs22 Later, the Three Sisters, as Eloise christened them, were in the kitchen making sandwiches.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93It gives me the creeps in there.\'94 Kathy emerged from the walk-in stasis closet with a thick wooden board bearing a large chunk of pastrami that could have been in there since the Ottoman Empire. \'93How do we know this isn't from the arse of the last guest who accidentally got locked in there?\'94\
\'93I think I\'92ll have the smoked salmon,\'94 Hayley said, stopping mid-stroke of slicing the vintage artisan sourdough. 
\f2\i Guaranteed at least twenty years old
\f1\i0  was baked into the bottom of the loaf.\
\'93Well, I\'92m not going back in there,\'94 Kathy said. \'93There\'92s no way I want to end up in someone\'92s casserole in a hundred years.\'94\
Sarah giggled as she tossed a fruit salad. \'93I would never think a scientist like you would be so superstitious,\'94 she said to Kathy.\
\'93I\'92d rather eat something pulled straight off a plant, thank you,\'94 Kathy defended.\
\'93Or smoke something pulled straight off a plant,\'94 Sarah observed, and gave Hayley side-eye. Hayley shrugged.\
\'93Say what you like; there\'92s no such thing as a free lunch,\'94 Kathy said.\
\'93This one certainly has strings attached,\'94 Hayley said as she finished sawing off a slice of bread with exaggerated vigour. She moved the butter crock in front of her. \'93Will Eloise ever let us out of here?\'94\
\'93No, I\'92m serious,\'94 Kathy asserted.\
Sarah blinked, sensing a rant was coming. Kathy had never ranted before.\
\'93There\'92s always some hideous price to pay. Take farming\'97the first hope for a free lunch ever conceived, the hope we could feed our bellies more reliably. Where did that get us?\'94\
\'93Fed?\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93Oh, I dunno,\'94 Sarah mused. \'93We got cities out of it?\'94\
Kathy pounced. \'93Exactly! And what was the basis of civilisation? Property rights. Suddenly people claimed they owned the ground on which every living thing depended.\'94\
Hayley paused, butter knife hovering. She glanced at Sarah. Sarah shrugged.\
\'93The farming free lunch gave us serfdom and slavery, the Divine Right of Kings, holiday homes, time-sharing\'85 Destroying natural habitat for the sake of a box of cereal is the original Original Sin.\'94\
Hayley set down her knife. She\'92d stopped following Kathy\'92s logic somewhere around time-sharing, but the intensity in Kathy\'92s voice suggested this wasn\'92t a good moment to ask for clarification.\
\'93Then there was the Industrial Revolution,\'94 Kathy forged on. \'93No free lunch there, especially for the children they sent down the coal mines.\'94\
\'93Um,\'94 Hayley ventured, \'93should I boil the kettle?\'94\
\'93Kettle?\'94 Kathy demanded. \'93Kettle! There\'92s no free lunch there either. I mean, do you think we can generate power for sipping tea, and warming our towel rails, and powering signage for products that give us cancer and obesity and attention deficits\'97\'94\
Sarah quietly asked Hayley, \'93Do you think this needs sugar?\'94\
\'93Sugar?\'94 Kathy exploded. \'93Don\'92t get me started on sugar. Blows every cell in your body up to the size of a weather balloon if you eat enough\'85\'94 She took a breath. \'93Tax, I need a patch.\'94\
Hayley assembled sandwiches while Sarah retrieved tea leaves from the stasis closet.\
\'93Don\'92t stay in there too long,\'94 Kathy warned. \'93Or you\'92ll be desiccated tea leaves before long.\'94\
Hayley glanced at the stasis closet door, then at Sarah\'92s retreating back, then at Kathy. She was starting to piece together where this was going, and the professional part of her brain was trying very hard to pretend this was the sort of thing that happened to other detectives in other, more interesting jurisdictions.\
\'93Doesn\'92t anyone find stasis technology just a little suspicious?\'94 Kathy continued. \'93I mean, where are the consequences? There\'92s always consequences. Always. Show me one technology that doesn\'92t come with such dire consequence that we find ourselves forced to devise an even worse technology for cleaning up the mess the first one caused.\'94\
Hayley\'92s knife stilled mid-sandwich. She was listening properly now.\
\'93We got a problem? Mice eating our grain stores? Just poison the tax out of them. What could possibly go wrong? Poison in your cereal? Eat bacon and eggs instead. Hover-copts won\'92t stay in the air because no one understands the physics of how they get off the ground and no one can competently fly them? Just add artificial intelligence. You know hover-copts crash more now than ever, right?\'94\
Sarah emerged from the stasis closet. Hayley put the sandwiches on plates. Sarah tossed measured spoonfuls of tea into a pot.\
Kathy panted and fanned her face with hummingbird-fast hands. \'93So in a world full of taxed technology designed to make the greedy greedier at any price, what\'92s the one stand-out technology that seems just so perfect, the one that literally delivers a fresh free lunch every day of the week?\'94\
Hayley\'92s eyebrows drew together. \'93Stasis tech,\'94 Sarah said, just moments ahead of her.\
\'93Yahtzee!\'94 Kathy exclaimed. \'93And Sarah gets a free pickle with her pastrami.\'94\
\'93Well, sure,\'94 Hayley said, over a pang of envy about Sarah\'92s looks. \'93We\'92ve had stasis fridges since before we were born. They\'92re safe.\'94\
\'93They\'92re safe? They\'92re safe?\'94 Kathy\'92s voice rose. \'93Haven\'92t you been listening? There\'92s. No. Free. Lunch.\'94 When neither of the others answered, she added, \'93Where\'92s the consequences?\'94\
Sarah shuffled on her feet. Hayley looked equally unsettled. As Airborne, a consideration of consequences never entered into anything. Life was demonstrably without them.\
\'93How about you tell us?\'94 Hayley challenged, though her voice had lost some of its certainty. If Kathy was onto something\'97if that technology was in every stasis unit in the country\'97\
\'93Well, I don\'92t know either, obviously,\'94 Kathy admitted, \'93but there has to be some, right? How can stasis tech oppose entropy without some serious voodoo behind it? I mean, any seriously advanced technology must have some Faustian bargain behind it, right? Who\'92s to say\'97I dunno\'97there\'92s not one of those horrendous Gyges Discs in every fridge in the land?\'94\
\'93What?\'94 Sarah demanded. \'93They\'92re weapons. They make reality shake.\'94 Sarah hadn\'92t thought of the disc\'92s effects like that before, but it resonated for her.\
\'93And maybe they make time shake too,\'94 Kathy countered. \'93Or stand still. Isn\'92t that the very essence of stasis?\'94\
Hayley\'92s blood chilled. She looked at the stasis closet\'92s humming door. Then back at Kathy. Then at the sandwiches on their plates, which had come from that closet.\
\'93Sure,\'94 Kathy asserted. \'93Maybe fridges are powered by some cosmic hell-engine. By the same thing that causes memory losses and time jumps? Maybe this pastrami just keeps forgetting how old it is.\'94\
\'93Kathy,\'94 Sarah soothed. \'93Maybe you really should use a patch.\'94\
\'93I ran out days ago! When is Eloise going to bring me my taxing patches?\'94\
\'93Look, take a seat, okay?\'94\
\'93Is it so hard to believe that the one perfect technology isn\'92t so perfect after all? Taxing hell. If it\'92s so benign, why is there a Food Preservation Secrets Act? An act of parliament, for tax sake.\'94\
\'93Well that\'92s easy,\'94 Sarah said with a benign sense of world order. \'93It\'92s to protect the Intellectual Property of\'97\'94\
\'93See?\'94 Kathy demanded. \'93Here we are again, protecting the people who stole the land for farms in the first place. You can\'92t sing the virtues of IP law without acknowledging its darker side.\'94\
Sarah was becoming indignant, in spite of herself. \'93Oh? And what\'92s that?\'94\
\'93The same laws that protect Intellectual\'97I won\'92t use the \'92P\'92 word again\'97are equally good at covering up Intellectual Stupidity.\'94\
Sarah blinked. Hayley stood mutely by, her investigative mind processing at speed. Memory losses. Time jumps. Gyges Disc phenomena. If every stasis unit had one\'97\
\'93Take a look at the world and tell me you don\'92t see stupidity everywhere,\'94 Kathy said. \'93But somehow this one perfect, benign technology is the exception to all that?\'94\
\'93Kathy, please\'85\'94\
\'93Okay,\'94 Kathy said with a wild grin on her face. \'93If you think that stasis closet is so safe, how about you both spend the night in there, and we\'92ll see how perfect you both look in the morning. Well, not you, Sarah, you always look perfect.\'94\
Sarah self-consciously pat her stomach; Hayley brushed her eyebrow straight and hoped her make-up was holding.\
\'93What? No takers?\'94 Wide-eyed, Kathy yanked a drawer open and snatched a knife. \'93I\'92ll prove it to you.\'94 Within a second, she\'92d flung open the stasis closet\'92s wide door and marched inside.\
Hayley and Sarah followed. \'93What are you doing?\'94\
\'93I\'92m gonna open up this thing and see how it works.\'94\
\'93What?\'94 Hayley and Sarah competed to look more alarmed than the other.\
\'93What if stasis tech is powered by Gyges Discs? 
\f2\i And
\f1\i0  they\'92re weapons? Call me crazy, but that seems exactly on point for the way this world works.\'94\
Kathy careened around the shelves and stacked crates, looking for the right thing to crack open.\
\'93No. Kathy, no,\'94 Hayley pleaded as she and Sarah grabbed for Kathy\'92s shoulders. Kathy, with the greater head of steam and momentum, shrugged them off easily. \'93You\'92ll get us all arrested,\'94 Hayley said with both authority and a trace of panic in her voice.\
Kathy discovered a sign reading 
\fs18 NO SERVICEABLE PARTS INSIDE
\fs22  on a panel about chest high on the rear wall. When Hayley and Sarah pounced again, she was already trying to prise the panel away.\
They tumbled to the floor in a tangle of limbs. Kathy squirmed away, exploiting their indecision about what they\'92d actually do if they caught her. For over a minute, a clumsy ballet of grasping hands and muffled protests played out across the floor, until they all lay exhausted, their wrestling dissolved into an awkward three-way heap.\
Kathy\'92s head lay on Sarah\'92s thigh. Sarah waited for her to move. Kathy\'92s breathing had steadied, but she remained perfectly still. Sarah\'92s hand hovered uncertainly above Kathy\'92s shoulder, acutely aware of the warmth and weight of her head, the intimacy of the position, and the fact that Kathy showed no signs of moving.\
Hayley got to her feet first and offered Kathy a hand up.
